From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] What holds the previous screen is the animation, not the
 visibility

Patches 0069 and 0070 were both run on the device (2026-11-03,
eng.builde.20261103.024249) and the screen was still black. This time the log
0070 added said what was happening, which is what it was for:

    PW3: the deadline passed with nothing to show; keeping 1 screen(s)
         until 1 application(s) draw

So the hold was taken, `dumpsys window windows` showed the launcher in
mHeldOverClosingApps, its surface still existed and its client still wanted it
visible (mViewVisibility=0x0) -- and isOnScreen was false all the same.

What WindowState.isOnScreen() reads:

    (!isParentWindowHidden() && !atoken.hiddenRequested)
        || mWinAnimator.mAnimation != null
        || atoken.mAppAnimator.animation != null

Measured on the device before the deadline, while the launcher was on screen:
hidden=false, hiddenRequested=true, mClientHidden=false. hiddenRequested is set
from the start of the transition, so the first clause is false the whole time
and the third is what holds the previous screen up. That animation is
AppWindowAnimator.sDummyAnimation -- a flag, not a cost: getTransformation()
returns false, and stepAnimationLocked() returns before doing anything with the
comment "count it as not animating for purposes of scheduling transactions".

handleClosingApps() clears it with appAnimator.setNullAnimation() three lines
above where 0069 put its decision. So 0069 skipped the hiding and the screen
emptied anyway, from a line it had read past.

This moves the decision above that call: a held application keeps its dummy
animation, and releasing it clears the animation before hiding, which is the
other half of the same thing.

What this says about the design, now that the mechanism is known: holding the
screen past the deadline is the same act as a longer deadline (patch 0067), done
to one transition instead of all of them. That is a narrower change, not a
different one -- which is the honest reading of what 0067 and this do together,
and is worth saying because the first version of this patch was written as
though it were doing something else.

Not run on the device yet when this patch was written.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/WindowSurfacePlacer.java
@@ -459,7 +459,30 @@
             final AppWindowAnimator appAnimator = wtoken.mAppAnimator;
             if (DEBUG_APP_TRANSITIONS) Slog.v(TAG, "Now closing app " + wtoken);
             appAnimator.clearThumbnail();
-            appAnimator.setNullAnimation();
+            if (!holdOver) {
+                // What keeps a window on screen while a transition is pending
+                // is this animation, not its visibility. WindowState.isOnScreen()
+                // reads
+                //
+                //     (!isParentWindowHidden() && !atoken.hiddenRequested)
+                //         || mWinAnimator.mAnimation != null
+                //         || atoken.mAppAnimator.animation != null
+                //
+                // and hiddenRequested is already true from the start of the
+                // transition (measured on the device, 2026-11-03: the outgoing
+                // launcher reads hidden=false hiddenRequested=true and is on
+                // screen), so the first clause is false and the third is what
+                // holds it. The animation is AppWindowAnimator.sDummyAnimation,
+                // which is a flag and not a cost: getTransformation() returns
+                // false and stepAnimationLocked() returns before doing anything
+                // ("count it as not animating for purposes of scheduling
+                // transactions").
+                //
+                // Patch 0069 skipped the hiding below and left this line alone,
+                // so the screen went black anyway and the patch looked like it
+                // was not in the image.
+                appAnimator.setNullAnimation();
+            }
             // TODO: Do we need to add to mNoAnimationNotifyOnTransitionFinished like above if not
             //       animating?
             if (holdOver) {
@@ -618,6 +641,8 @@
                 // placement that is about to lay out anyway, and asking for
                 // another one from in here calls performSurfacePlacement()
                 // before mInLayout is set, which is a recursive traversal.
+                // The dummy animation held it; clearing it is half of letting go.
+                wtoken.mAppAnimator.setNullAnimation();
                 wtoken.setVisibility(null, false, AppTransition.TRANSIT_UNSET,
                         false /* performLayout */, wtoken.mVoiceInteraction);
                 wtoken.updateReportedVisibilityLocked();
